WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.400
SPEAKER 1: After building
an API with Apigee

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.110
and sharing it with
developers, there's

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.110
still work to be done to
make sure that everyone

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.920
has a successful experience.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.950
As an API producer, you may want
to know critical information

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.830
like how is my API traffic
trending over time,

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.790
when is API response
time fastest or slowest,

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:18.930
and who are my top developers?

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.960
The answers to these questions
can help you improve your APIs,

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.000
troubleshoot problems,
and make better decisions

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.690
for your business.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.200
This is where Apigee
Analytics comes into play.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.300
Apigee Analytics
collects and calculates

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.270
a wealth of information that
flows through API proxies.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.160
You can visualize this
data in the Apigee

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.660
UI with predefined
analytics dashboards,

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.750
as well as custom reports.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.180
Apigee Analytics can help
improve the API experience

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.480
for everyone.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.990
This data can help provide
insight to the API team

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.390
about how the API
program is doing overall,

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.090
how individual APIs are
doing, and how to improve

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.950
and debug APIs.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.570
Analytics information
can also be

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.580
shared with app
developers, who will

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.820
want to know how to improve
their apps to increase revenue.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.580
The operations team can learn
more about traffic patterns

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.410
and when to add
back-end resources

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.510
or make other adjustments.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.460
And the business owner
can see whether or not

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.250
their initial API
investment is paying off.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.520
In other episodes, we learned
how to build an API proxy,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.510
secure APIs with API keys,
and set up a developer portal.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.182
In this episode, we'll take
a tour of Apigee Analytics

00:01:21.182 --> 00:01:23.640
and learn how it can help you
troubleshoot customer issues.

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:33.070
First, visit apigee.google.com
to open the Apigee UI.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:36.130
As you can see, we've already
created some API proxies

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.140
and generated some
traffic against them.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.810
In the left Navigation bar,
click Analyze, and then

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.720
API Metrics.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.540
This shows you Apigee's
pre-built analytics dashboards

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.630
for API proxy performance,
cache performance, error code

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:53.020
analysis, latency analysis,
and target performance.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.330
API proxy performance
gives you information

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.990
about the performance of your
proxy over a period of time.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.120
You can see how much
overall traffic you've had,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.510
including whether it resulted
in success or failure.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:08.410
There's also average response
time in milliseconds.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:12.190
You can also view analytics
for a specific proxy.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.230
Next up, there's
cache performance,

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.170
where you can see the cache hit
rate, the number of cache hits,

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.335
and cache response times.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.117
You can also take a look
at error code analysis,

00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:26.200
where you can learn about
how many errors occurred

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.658
and what types of
errors they were.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.780
Here we have 60 errors that
were 404, meaning not found.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.630
There were also 30 errors that
were 401, meaning unauthorized.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:43.630
These likely happened
because the caller did not

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.330
provide a valid API key.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.580
Now, let's take a look
at latency analysis.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:56.780
First, select a time
range of the last hour.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.210
We'll select an API
proxy to look at.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:02.150
Here we can see some
statistics about response time.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:05.270
At 3 o'clock in the
afternoon, the 99th percentile

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:09.260
was 97 milliseconds, meaning
that 99% of API calls

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.020
were faster than
97 milliseconds.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.480
We can also see that
95% of API calls

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.820
were faster than
14 milliseconds.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.010
And of course, the
median response time

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.290
was just two milliseconds.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:23.810
You can analyze response
time by the target as well as

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.380
requests and response
processing latency.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.270
There's also a Target
Performance dashboard

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.860
to see how fast the
back end system is

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.755
responding to your requests.

00:03:32.755 --> 00:03:34.130
This can help you
understand when

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:35.380
you might want to use caching.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.450
Beyond API metrics,
you can also learn more

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:44.120
about the developers
that are using your APIs.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.570
Under Developers, can
click Developer Engagement.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:49.820
Here you can see the total
number of developers,

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:51.740
the number of
developers with apps,

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.875
and the number of
active developers.

00:03:53.875 --> 00:03:57.050
You can also browse details
about individual applications

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.410
the developers have registered
using the Developer portal.

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:01.880
Here we have two apps--

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:06.140
one for stackdriver monitoring
and another for ping stats.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.690
For each of these apps, we can
see the email of the developer

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:12.380
as well as traffic
and error statistics.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:14.510
There's also a traffic
composition section,

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.089
where you can take a look
at your top 10 API proxies

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.279
by amount of traffic.

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:22.430
In this case, the vast
majority of API proxy traffic

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:26.000
is for ping status V1.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.190
You can also take a look
at your top 10 developers

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:31.490
by amount of traffic, top 10
products by amount of traffic,

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:35.360
and top 10 apps by
amount of traffic.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.220
There's also an
End Users section,

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:39.020
where you can see the
types of user agents

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:41.570
that are accessing an API proxy.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.150
In this case, we have
some cURL calls as well as

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.635
some library calls via Postman.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:51.170
Finally, there's a geo-map
that allows us to see

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:53.450
traffic broken down by country.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.130
Here we can see that
most of our traffic

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.870
is coming from
the United States,

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:59.450
but we also have some traffic
from Singapore, Belgium,

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:01.880
and Brazil.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:04.400
In addition to the pre-built
Analytics dashboards,

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:05.945
you can also create
custom reports.

00:05:10.633 --> 00:05:12.050
Here we have a
custom report where

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:15.670
we can see traffic broken
down by application.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.410
If you select an
application, you

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:19.870
can also see the breakdown
of traffic by API call.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:25.400
You can also expand the time
frame beyond the current day.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.610
If you want to expand beyond
the listed maximum of 14 days,

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.890
you can submit a job to
handle report generation

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:32.767
asynchronously in
the background.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:38.750
You can come back later to
see the results of this custom

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:39.250
report.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.910
As you can see, Apigee Analytics
comes packed with helpful data

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:47.320
visualizations.

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:49.390
One key use case for
these visualizations

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:50.770
is troubleshooting.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:52.750
Data visualization
is a powerful way

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.720
to unlock, compare,
contrast, and assess

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:59.710
the underlying data that Apigee
collects about your APIs.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.410
Perhaps you've recently received
some feedback from a customer

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:05.470
that one of your
APIs is being slow.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:07.270
Apigee Analytics can
help you figure out

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:09.070
exactly what's going on.

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:11.530
You can check to see if the
slowness is being seen by just

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.540
one app or multiple apps.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:15.340
If it's just being
seen by one app,

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:18.040
there may be a
problem with that app.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.410
You can also look to see if
the slowness is concentrated

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:24.640
in a certain geolocation, which
may suggest a network problem.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.650
If neither of these
is the case, then you

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:28.570
might have a problem
with Apigee and may

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:31.090
want to look at
policy configuration.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.580
It may also be helpful to
compare the average response

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:35.980
time of different APIs
to see if one of them

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:39.080
is significantly
underperforming the others.

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:41.440
And finally, you could
generate a custom report

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:45.890
to visualize maximum latency
time by app and resource.

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:48.550
Thanks for joining us to learn
more about Apigee Analytics--

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:50.590
a powerful tool to
help you visualize

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:52.780
API data to gain
insights that can help

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.640
drive business success.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:57.083
And friends, if you found
this episode helpful,

00:06:57.083 --> 00:06:59.500
please click the Like button
and subscribe to the channel.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:01.050
Cheers.

